course: everyday
lesson: 109
title: Baths, water at home and doors
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Water at home can scald, make floors slippery, block pipes and flood rooms, so a few simple habits keep everyone safe and dry.
explain: Hot tap water can be hot enough to scald, which is why you run cold first and test the water with your elbow, which feels heat better than your hand.
explain: Doors are heavy levers: the hinge side crushes fingers, slammed doors trap hands and break glass, and fire doors hold back smoke.
explain: For example, a boy in a block of flats in Glasgow who sees a fire door propped open closes it, because it stops smoke spreading along the corridor.
fact: Run cold water first, then add hot, and test the bath with your elbow before getting in.
fact: Step onto a mat and hold the side when getting out of the bath, because wet surfaces are very slippery.
fact: If a bath or sink overflows, turn off the tap, pull the plug, keep away from electrical things and tell a grown-up.
fact: Water from an overflowing bath can leak through the floor into the ceiling below and onto electrical wiring.
fact: A dripping tap wastes a lot of water over days, so tell a grown-up.
fact: Empty buckets after use, because a toddler can fall headfirst into one and drown.
fact: If the toilet is blocked, stop flushing, because flushing again makes it overflow.
fact: Only toilet paper goes down the toilet, never wipes (even ones labelled flushable), toys or cotton buds, because they do not break up and they block the pipes.
fact: If a pipe bursts, tell a grown-up at once; turning off the stopcock or main water valve stops the flood.
fact: Close doors gently, because slamming can trap fingers, break glass and scare people.
fact: Check around a door before closing it, because the hinge side crushes fingers badly.
fact: Open doors slowly and look through any glass, so you do not hit someone on the other side.
fact: Little children should not lock the bathroom door, so that grown-ups can get in if they are ill or stuck.
fact: Fire doors must stay closed to stop smoke and fire spreading.
fact: Slow down near glass doors and look for stickers on them, because people walk into clean glass and it can break and cut.
exercise: You are running a bath. What is the safe way? | Run cold first, add hot, and test with your elbow | Run only the hot water so the bath gets nice and warm | Jump in and then add cold water | Test it by putting your foot straight in, then jump in
exercise: Why test bath water with your elbow? | Elbows feel heat better than hands | Elbows cannot be burnt | Elbows are much cleaner than your hands | Hands are too small to tell
exercise: How do you get out of the bath safely? | Step onto a mat and hold the side | Jump out onto the tiles | Climb out standing on the edge | Run out quickly before you get cold
exercise: The bathroom sink is overflowing. What do you do? | Turn off the tap, pull the plug and tell a grown-up | Let it run and fetch a towel | Unplug the hair dryer quickly with your wet hands first | Leave the room and close the door
exercise: Why can an overflowing bath be dangerous? | Water can leak through the ceiling onto electrical wiring | It makes the water in the pipes hot | It makes the bath tap stop working for good | It only wastes soap
exercise: A tap in the kitchen keeps dripping. What should you do? | Tell a grown-up so it can be fixed | Leave it, as a few little drips waste nothing at all | Turn it on fully to clean it | Put a cup under it and forget it
exercise: A bucket of water is left in the garden and your toddler cousin is playing nearby. What do you do? | Empty the bucket | Put a toy in it to play with | Leave it, as it is not deep | Move it nearer to the toddler
exercise: The toilet is blocked and the water is rising. What do you do? | Stop flushing and tell a grown-up | Flush it again a few times to push it all through | Flush three times quickly | Pour more water into it
exercise: Which of these can go down the toilet? | Only toilet paper | Wet wipes labelled flushable | Cotton buds | Small toys
exercise: Water is pouring through the ceiling from a burst pipe. What is the most important thing? | Tell a grown-up at once so they can turn off the water | Try to fix the burst pipe yourself with some tape | Put a bowl under it and wait | Turn on all the taps in the house
exercise: You are angry and want to slam your bedroom door. Why is that a bad idea? | It can trap fingers, break glass and scare people | It makes the door open more easily next time you use it | It is fine if nobody is home | It makes the door stronger
exercise: Your little sister's fingers are near the hinge side of the door. What do you do? | Check and move her hand before closing it | Close it quickly before she moves | Close it slowly while she is still holding on to it | Tell her and close it anyway
exercise: There is a swing door into the school hall. How should you open it? | Slowly, looking through the glass first | With a big hard push to swing it right open | By running into it with your shoulder | By kicking it open
exercise: Your five-year-old brother wants to lock the bathroom door. What is the problem? | Grown-ups may need to get in if he is ill or stuck | He might forget where the toilet is in the house | The lock will wear out too fast | Locked doors make the room colder
exercise: A fire door in your block of flats is propped open with a chair. What should you do? | Move the chair and let it close | Leave it, as it is easier to walk through | Prop the other fire doors open too | Lock the fire door shut
exercise: A patio door is so clean it looks open. What should you do? | Slow down and look for stickers on it | Run through it quickly | Walk straight through it with your eyes closed | Push it with your head
test: Chloe in Phoenix runs a bath with only the hot tap. Why is that risky? | Hot tap water can scald her | Hot water makes the bath overflow faster | Hot water is dirtier than cold | The bath will crack from hot water
test: Why is a bucket of water dangerous to a toddler? | A toddler can fall headfirst in and drown | The bucket can tip over and get the toddler wet | Toddlers might drink the water | The water attracts insects
test: What happens if you keep flushing a blocked toilet? | It overflows onto the floor | It clears the block every time | It makes the water go cleaner | The toilet switches itself off
test: Why do wet wipes cause problems in pipes? | They do not break up and they block the pipes | They make the water too soapy | They make pipes too slippery | They turn into toilet paper once they are in water
test: Which is true about doors in a fire? | Closed fire doors stop smoke and fire spreading | Open doors let the smoke escape safely | Fire doors are only for decoration | All doors should be wedged open in a fire so people can get out
test: Ethan swings a door open fast and hits his friend. What should he do next time? | Open doors slowly and look through any glass | Open doors only with his foot | Shout before opening every door | Run through every door quickly so that it shuts fast behind him
test: Why should a grown-up know where the stopcock is? | It turns off the water to stop a flood | It turns off all the electricity in the house | It switches the heating on | It makes the water hotter
test: Which is the safest way to step out of the shower? | Onto a bath mat, holding a rail or the side | Straight onto the wet tiles in bare feet, quickly | Jumping onto the bedroom carpet | Sliding out on one foot
test: A little girl in Dublin's fingers got hurt by a closing door. Which side most likely crushed them? | The hinge side | The handle side | The top of the door | The bottom of the door
test: running my bath, hot tap first yeah? | No, cold first, then add hot and test with your elbow | Yes, hot first so it warms up quicker | Doesn't matter which, just test it with your toes before u get in | Hot only, then wait for it to cool
test: toilet's blocked, flush again?? | No, stop flushing and tell a grown-up | Yes, flush harder a few more times till it goes | Yes, and pour in more paper | Flush and walk away
test: can i flush these wipes nan | No, only toilet paper goes down the toilet | Yes, wipes break up like paper | Yes, if you only flush a few | Yes, if they say flushable they never block
test: bath's overflowing and the hairdryers plugged in nearby | Turn off the tap, pull the plug, keep away from the hairdryer and tell a grown-up | Unplug the hairdryer with your wet hands first, then mop the floor and say nothing | Mop it up and say nothing | Leave it to drain away by itself and get out of the bathroom
explain: A toddler in Florida was left in the bath for a moment while his mum answered the phone; he slipped under, and she pulled him out coughing just in time.
explain: A boy in Leeds slammed a door on his sister's fingers during a game; her fingertip was crushed and she needed a hospital visit.
fact: Small children can drown in a few centimetres of water in seconds and without a sound.
fact: Door hinges and slammed doors cause many finger injuries to small children.
test: watching lil bro in the bath, gonna grab my phone quick | Stay with him, he can drown in seconds | It's fine for a minute, he's a good swimmer anyway | Ask him to shout if he slips | Leave the water running
test: we play slam the door game | Stop, doors can crush fingers badly | Slam it softer, then it's fine | Only slam it on big kids | Play it in the dark
test: bath water feels super hot, its fine im tough | Check it and cool it, hot water can scald | Get in fast so it doesn't hurt as much | Add bubbles to cool it | Only babies get scalded
test: did u hear, a toddler slipped under in the bath when his mum went to get the phone, they say he screamed the house down. what's probably wrong | The screaming; small children drown quickly and silently | That a toddler can slip under the water in a bath at all, because it never happens | That it happened while his mum was away | That a little water can be dangerous
test: my cousin says Amy slammed the door on her sister's fingers n the finger came right off n they sewed it back on. what's the real lesson | Close doors gently and check around the hinge side first | Slam doors fast so fingers have no time to get caught | Only glass doors can hurt fingers when shut | Doors are only dangerous when they are locked and someone pushes them
test: Ethan flushed a toy down the loo n flooded the whole flat downstairs, everyone's calling him Toilet Boy, wanna join in? | No, nicknames hurt; the lesson is only toilet paper goes down | Yes, because the nickname is really funny and everyone will love it | Yes, but only when Ethan is not around | Yes, so he never does it again
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the temperature of bath water is tested with the elbow. | The elbow perceives heat better than the hand | The elbow is less easily wetted by the water | The hand is liable to be scalded at any temperature | The elbow cools the water as it is inserted
exam: Whereas a fire door is found propped open, determine the appropriate action. | Close it, as it prevents the spread of smoke and fire | Leave it open so as to admit fresh air | Wedge it more firmly so that it remains open for everybody | Remove it from its hinges and inform no one
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning small children and shallow water. | They may drown in a few centimetres within seconds | They are safe in water below the knee of an adult | They will always cry out loudly when they are in any difficulty | They cannot drown where they are able to stand
exam: Pursuant to the overflow of a bath, ascertain the correct procedure. | Turn off the tap, pull the plug and avoid electrical things | Leave the tap running and mop the floor beneath it | Switch on an electric heater nearby so as to dry the floor quickly | Unplug the bathroom light while standing in water
